WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:13.485
&gt;&gt;&gt; Well, this is what I was

00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:15.787
excited for.

00:00:16.155 --> 00:00:19.491
Our next guest's dream is to

00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:20.926
become an astrophysicist.

00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:23.295
Taking the steps towards that

00:00:23.428 --> 00:00:23.595
dream.

00:00:23.729 --> 00:00:24.963
He finished high school in under

00:00:25.097 --> 00:00:30.969
three years with a 4.0 GPA and

00:00:31.103 --> 00:00:31.670
finished a nester of college.

00:00:31.803 --> 00:00:32.304
Here is the I can kicker.

00:00:32.437 --> 00:00:34.173
He is 9.

00:00:34.173 --> 00:00:34.306
That is him.

00:00:34.439 --> 00:00:38.911
In addition being one of the

00:00:39.044 --> 00:00:39.645
youngest high school graduates in

00:00:39.778 --> 00:00:44.983
the company, he is a member of

00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:46.185
mensa.

00:00:46.185 --> 00:00:47.719
His parents join us now.

00:00:47.853 --> 00:00:49.221
My goodness.

00:00:49.354 --> 00:00:50.189
&gt;&gt; Good morning!

00:00:50.189 --> 00:00:52.357
&gt;&gt; Hey, David.

00:00:52.491 --> 00:00:53.025
How is college?

00:00:53.158 --> 00:00:57.696
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:00:59.464
&gt;&gt; What was it like graduating

00:00:59.598 --> 00:01:07.406
from high school at 9?

00:01:07.539 --> 00:01:08.207
&gt;&gt; Well, everybody started

00:01:08.207 --> 00:01:11.243
talking about how this is an

00:01:11.376 --> 00:01:12.678
incredible accomplishment, and I

00:01:12.811 --> 00:01:16.348
was thinking how all I did was

00:01:16.481 --> 00:01:18.483
graduate.

00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:20.886
And then my mom saying, yes, you

00:01:21.019 --> 00:01:24.223
graduated at the age of 9, and

00:01:24.223 --> 00:01:25.557
then I say, yeah, I graduated.

00:01:25.691 --> 00:01:26.625
What's the big deal?

00:01:26.758 --> 00:01:28.360
You graduated at the age of 9.

00:01:28.493 --> 00:01:31.330
So what is the big deal?

00:01:31.463 --> 00:01:33.198
&gt;&gt; So what is the big deal?

00:01:33.332 --> 00:01:34.533
Do you know what the big deal

00:01:34.666 --> 00:01:36.368
is?

00:01:36.501 --> 00:01:40.339
This is normal to you?

00:01:40.472 --> 00:01:41.773
&gt;&gt; It just comes.

00:01:41.907 --> 00:01:44.243
You just, for me, you just do

00:01:44.243 --> 00:01:44.376
it.

00:01:44.376 --> 00:01:46.545
&gt;&gt; So, mom and dad, what did you

00:01:46.678 --> 00:01:46.812
do?

00:01:46.945 --> 00:01:47.746
Every parent watching is like,

00:01:47.879 --> 00:01:49.281
wait a minute, what did they do

00:01:49.414 --> 00:01:51.617
and what am I doing wrong?

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.953
Did you do anything special, you

00:01:55.087 --> 00:01:55.787
think, or different that puts

00:01:55.921 --> 00:01:59.591
David ahead of the pack?

00:01:59.725 --> 00:02:02.261
&gt;&gt; I think you just have to

00:02:02.261 --> 00:02:04.263
listen to your child and get

00:02:04.363 --> 00:02:04.997
outside of the box.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.732
He was the one leading the

00:02:06.865 --> 00:02:07.065
charge.

00:02:07.199 --> 00:02:08.500
He is the one that was

00:02:08.634 --> 00:02:08.867
motivated.

00:02:09.001 --> 00:02:10.836
I just had to break out of the

00:02:10.969 --> 00:02:12.504
box and think outside of the

00:02:12.638 --> 00:02:15.173
normal schooling, think outside

00:02:15.307 --> 00:02:17.776
of what people know and what I

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:20.979
know growing up and just give

00:02:21.113 --> 00:02:22.781
him a chance to be and do what

00:02:22.914 --> 00:02:24.283
he is dreaming to be and do.

00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:27.319
&gt;&gt; What could you mean by that?

00:02:27.452 --> 00:02:27.686
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:30.956
Go ahead, dad.

00:02:31.089 --> 00:02:31.390
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:02:31.523 --> 00:02:32.324
&gt;&gt; In addition to that, there

00:02:32.457 --> 00:02:36.895
are so many factors that we saw

00:02:37.029 --> 00:02:38.830
very early when David was

00:02:38.964 --> 00:02:41.700
growing up.

00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:44.803
He is the type that is very

00:02:44.936 --> 00:02:49.174
curious about everything.

00:02:49.308 --> 00:02:51.810
And everywhere we went, he

00:02:51.943 --> 00:02:53.045
always asked, what is this?

00:02:53.178 --> 00:02:54.313
What is that?

00:02:54.313 --> 00:02:57.015
And then if you explain and you

00:02:57.149 --> 00:02:58.917
use big words, he will want you

00:02:59.051 --> 00:03:00.852
to break it down and it he will

00:03:00.986 --> 00:03:04.323
me what does that mean.

00:03:04.323 --> 00:03:07.592
And when he comes with me to my

00:03:07.726 --> 00:03:10.495
clinic, I have an outpatient

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:11.029
psychiatric clinic.

00:03:11.163 --> 00:03:13.098
When he comes to me, I mean, to

00:03:13.231 --> 00:03:17.536
the clinic, he always asks

00:03:17.669 --> 00:03:17.903
questions.

00:03:18.337 --> 00:03:22.341
Why do you do this as opposed to

00:03:22.341 --> 00:03:22.541
doing that?

00:03:22.674 --> 00:03:27.346
And what does this mean?

00:03:27.479 --> 00:03:28.347
Instead of, you know.

00:03:28.347 --> 00:03:31.283
And you have to explain without

00:03:31.416 --> 00:03:32.684
explaining, if you know what I

00:03:32.818 --> 00:03:34.486
mean, because you still have to

00:03:34.619 --> 00:03:36.355
pay attention to the

00:03:36.488 --> 00:03:42.461
requirements of the law.

00:03:42.594 --> 00:03:44.363
We keep patients' information

00:03:44.363 --> 00:03:44.896
highly confidential because of

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:45.831
the hippa law.

00:03:45.964 --> 00:03:46.531
And I still have to break it

00:03:46.665 --> 00:03:49.134
down for him.

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:53.004
I never said to him, shut up!

00:03:53.138 --> 00:03:54.506
Or go and do something else.

00:03:54.639 --> 00:03:54.973
No.

00:03:55.107 --> 00:03:56.541
I would take the time to break

00:03:56.675 --> 00:03:56.942
this down.

00:03:57.075 --> 00:04:01.680
&gt;&gt; Dad, I am going to make this

00:04:01.813 --> 00:04:02.381
point with poppy.

00:04:02.381 --> 00:04:04.383
When I was a kid, my mom would

00:04:04.416 --> 00:04:08.754
pull me back, I'd go talk to

00:04:08.887 --> 00:04:10.021
my

00:04:10.389 --> 00:04:11.423
dad would say don't make him

00:04:11.556 --> 00:04:12.457
afraid of people.

00:04:12.591 --> 00:04:16.928
Let him be curious.

00:04:17.062 --> 00:04:18.463
You do the same thing.

00:04:18.597 --> 00:04:18.997
Don't tell them no.

00:04:19.131 --> 00:04:19.698
&gt;&gt; We can't do that now, but

00:04:19.831 --> 00:04:21.333
what else can we do.

00:04:21.466 --> 00:04:22.934
David, what do you want to be

00:04:23.068 --> 00:04:25.270
when you grow up, David?

00:04:25.404 --> 00:04:26.405
&gt;&gt; Well, there are a few things

00:04:26.405 --> 00:04:29.775
that I want to be.

00:04:29.908 --> 00:04:33.979
An astrophysicist, an engineer,

00:04:34.813 --> 00:04:39.584
a nuclear chemist, a software

00:04:39.718 --> 00:04:41.686
developer, to rocket engineer.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:48.660
What's the last one?

00:04:48.794 --> 00:04:49.261
Website designer.

00:04:49.394 --> 00:04:50.862
&gt;&gt; Nothing like -- a ballplayer

00:04:50.996 --> 00:04:52.097
would be great, too.

00:04:52.431 --> 00:04:54.433
He has all kinds of skills.

00:04:54.499 --> 00:04:55.867
Sure he could do it.

00:04:56.001 --> 00:04:57.536
David and mom and dad, we have a

00:04:57.669 --> 00:05:00.105
surprise for you, okay?

00:05:00.439 --> 00:05:00.539
Ready?

00:05:00.539 --> 00:05:00.772
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:05:00.906 --> 00:05:02.441
&gt;&gt; One of the world's most

00:05:02.441 --> 00:05:03.675
famous scientists wants to say

00:05:03.809 --> 00:05:06.812
hi to you.

00:05:06.945 --> 00:05:08.447
Astrophysicist Neil Degrasse

00:05:08.513 --> 00:05:11.049
Tyson is here with us.

00:05:11.183 --> 00:05:11.650
Hi, Neil, good morning!

00:05:11.783 --> 00:05:12.918
&gt;&gt; Are you serious?

00:05:13.051 --> 00:05:13.819
&gt;&gt; Hey, everybody.

00:05:13.952 --> 00:05:17.122
Hey!

00:05:17.255 --> 00:05:17.522
Hey, David.

00:05:17.656 --> 00:05:17.989
&gt;&gt; Good morning!

00:05:18.123 --> 00:05:20.459
&gt;&gt; How are you?

00:05:20.459 --> 00:05:21.760
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:05:21.893 --> 00:05:23.195
Hey, David, I was 9 when I

00:05:23.328 --> 00:05:28.033
decided I wanted to be an

00:05:28.467 --> 00:05:28.733
astrophysicist, except I was

00:05:28.867 --> 00:05:29.401
still in fourth grade, okay?

00:05:29.534 --> 00:05:32.838
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.971 --> 00:05:36.475
In case you didn't know this, it

00:05:36.475 --> 00:05:38.477
is unusual to be entering

00:05:38.477 --> 00:05:39.344
college at that grade.

00:05:39.478 --> 00:05:40.946
But the greatest value to

00:05:41.079 --> 00:05:42.481
knowing this early, what you

00:05:42.614 --> 00:05:44.483
might want to do, looks like you

00:05:44.483 --> 00:05:45.317
are giving yourself a few

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.118
options there, and that's great.

00:05:47.252 --> 00:05:49.554
The greatest value is you can

00:05:49.688 --> 00:05:52.491
align decisions related to your

00:05:52.491 --> 00:05:54.960
life to feed that interest and

00:05:55.093 --> 00:05:55.327
curiosity.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:58.763
So that by the time you become a

00:05:58.897 --> 00:06:01.533
professional in that world, your

00:06:01.666 --> 00:06:02.501
whole life has been invested on

00:06:02.501 --> 00:06:04.069
that path and then you will

00:06:04.503 --> 00:06:05.337
become better at that than

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.507
anything else you could imagine.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.509
So I am especially intrigued you

00:06:10.575 --> 00:06:12.110
know what you want to be when

00:06:12.511 --> 00:06:13.311
you are grown up.

00:06:13.445 --> 00:06:15.146
So you are kind of already grown

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:16.515
up, so I don't know what that

00:06:16.515 --> 00:06:16.681
means.

00:06:16.815 --> 00:06:18.683
&gt;&gt; David, is there anything you

00:06:18.817 --> 00:06:19.718
want to is say to the great Neil

00:06:19.851 --> 00:06:20.519
Degrasse Tyson before he with

00:06:20.552 --> 00:06:25.857
got to go?

00:06:25.991 --> 00:06:26.658
&gt;&gt; Well, we are really blessed

00:06:26.791 --> 00:06:31.396
to have you talk to David.

00:06:32.097 --> 00:06:36.535
I mean, he has read so much

00:06:36.535 --> 00:06:37.702
about you and heard so much

00:06:37.836 --> 00:06:43.008
about you and every time he sees

00:06:43.141 --> 00:06:48.547
you on the TV and doing

00:06:48.547 --> 00:06:49.014
something somewhere, he is

00:06:49.147 --> 00:06:49.781
always impressed.

00:06:49.915 --> 00:06:51.883
&gt;&gt; Dad, let him talk about it.

00:06:52.017 --> 00:06:52.551
&gt;&gt; To talk to you --

00:06:52.551 --> 00:06:54.553
&gt;&gt; Let him talk about it, dad.

00:06:54.586 --> 00:06:56.955
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:06:57.088 --> 00:06:57.489
&gt;&gt; How to you feel?

00:06:57.622 --> 00:07:00.025
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:07:00.158 --> 00:07:00.559
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:07:00.592 --> 00:07:02.561
&gt;&gt; How do you feel?

00:07:02.561 --> 00:07:05.463
&gt;&gt; So, I have created -- so I

00:07:05.597 --> 00:07:08.767
was in my Earth and science high

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:09.601
school class.

00:07:09.734 --> 00:07:10.702
I created a theory to refute

00:07:10.835 --> 00:07:11.803
"The big bang theory."

00:07:11.937 --> 00:07:17.008
&gt;&gt; Wow!

00:07:17.142 --> 00:07:17.576
&gt;&gt; We love new ideas.

00:07:17.709 --> 00:07:20.178
&gt;&gt; So the main -- thanks.

00:07:20.579 --> 00:07:20.912
&gt;&gt; Keep going.

00:07:21.046 --> 00:07:23.748
&gt;&gt; So the main part of the

00:07:23.882 --> 00:07:25.784
theory is that matter cannot be

00:07:25.917 --> 00:07:29.854
created or destroyed.

00:07:29.988 --> 00:07:33.391
According to the big

00:07:33.525 --> 00:07:36.595
bang theory, I guess you will

00:07:36.595 --> 00:07:40.165
call it bal cosmic eck,

00:07:40.599 --> 00:07:45.070
something like that, it was

00:07:45.203 --> 00:07:45.670
infinitely dense.

00:07:45.804 --> 00:07:49.374
In order for it to be infinitely

00:07:49.507 --> 00:07:51.176
dense, it has to be constantly

00:07:51.309 --> 00:07:51.676
creating matter.

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:52.143
&gt;&gt; Oh, my gosh.

00:07:52.277 --> 00:07:53.578
&gt;&gt; But in order for it to

00:07:53.712 --> 00:07:56.615
constantly be creating matter,

00:07:56.748 --> 00:07:58.283
it would have to violate the

00:07:58.617 --> 00:07:59.451
laws of physics.

00:07:59.584 --> 00:08:00.619
Since we are technically talking

00:08:00.685 --> 00:08:02.621
about the universe, not all the

00:08:02.654 --> 00:08:06.725
space outside of the universe,

00:08:06.858 --> 00:08:09.427
then the laws of physics should

00:08:09.561 --> 00:08:09.861
still apply.

00:08:09.995 --> 00:08:11.329
&gt;&gt; Wait.

00:08:11.463 --> 00:08:12.931
Neil, hang on one second.

00:08:13.064 --> 00:08:15.133
We have to go, sorry, David, we

00:08:15.266 --> 00:08:15.967
are just out of time.

00:08:16.101 --> 00:08:17.702
I don't mean to be

00:08:17.836 --> 00:08:20.639
disrespectleful from a

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:21.806
9-year-old who graduated

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:22.140
college.

00:08:22.273 --> 00:08:24.643
Neil, is he schooling you right

00:08:24.643 --> 00:08:24.743
now?

00:08:24.743 --> 00:08:27.479
We got to run.

00:08:27.612 --> 00:08:28.146
&gt;&gt;ING so we love new ideas.

00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:30.649
That challenge our previous

00:08:30.715 --> 00:08:30.882
ideas.

00:08:31.016 --> 00:08:31.616
In the end, David, come up with

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:34.052
a way to test your idea.

00:08:34.185 --> 00:08:35.620
Then we can then hand it over to

00:08:35.754 --> 00:08:37.656
people with telescopes and space

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:40.659
probes and this sort of thing

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:41.626
and we can see if your new

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.028
hypothesis about the universe is

00:08:43.161 --> 00:08:45.830
correct, that that's how we do

00:08:45.964 --> 00:08:46.131
that.

00:08:46.264 --> 00:08:47.599
That's how we roll on the

00:08:47.732 --> 00:08:47.966
frontier.

00:08:48.099 --> 00:08:49.067
&gt;&gt; We roll -- we have time.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:50.802
We have to deal with time issues

00:08:50.935 --> 00:08:52.671
here and get to breaks and pay

00:08:52.671 --> 00:08:52.871
the bills.

00:08:53.004 --> 00:08:53.938
We are so happy to have all of

00:08:54.072 --> 00:08:55.073
you on.

00:08:55.206 --> 00:08:58.176
David, best of luck to you.

00:08:58.309 --> 00:08:58.977
Mom and dad, as well.

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:00.145
Neil, very kind of you.

00:09:00.278 --> 00:09:01.479
&gt;&gt; My day has been made.

00:09:01.613 --> 00:09:04.315
&gt;&gt; By the way.

00:09:04.683 --> 00:09:04.749
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:09:04.883 --> 00:09:08.253
&gt;&gt; My podcast coming to the

00:09:08.687 --> 00:09:09.421
keswick theater in Pennsylvania

00:09:09.554 --> 00:09:12.123
outside of philly at the end of

00:09:12.257 --> 00:09:12.691
March.

00:09:12.691 --> 00:09:14.693
I would be delighted to offer

00:09:14.693 --> 00:09:15.894
your family some tickets for

00:09:16.027 --> 00:09:16.194
that.

00:09:16.327 --> 00:09:17.062
&gt;&gt; That would be great!

00:09:17.195 --> 00:09:19.364
&gt;&gt; That would be amazing.

00:09:19.497 --> 00:09:19.931
Thank you very much.

00:09:20.065 --> 00:09:21.766
&gt;&gt; We'd love to meet you all in

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:22.267
person.

00:09:22.701 --> 00:09:23.601
David, keep us abreast of what

00:09:23.735 --> 00:09:24.035
you are doing.

00:09:24.169 --> 00:09:24.703
Good luck.

